when approaching the architecture of  your data warehouse it's important to  understand the constructs of the  services you'll use  so what are folders projects and data  sets  and how do they come together to support  warehousing fundamentals  like security and cost management on  today's episode of bigquery spotlight  we're talking about the bigquery  resource model and giving you  our top tips for structuring your own  resources  let's get started by talking about how  bigquery fits into the google cloud  resource hierarchy  an organization is the root node of the  google cloud resource model  it represents a company probably the one  you work for  and it's linked to a domain through a  google workspace or cloud identity  account  an organization resource isn't required  to get started with bigquery  but it is recommended with an  organization  resources like bigquery datasets or  tables  can be centrally controlled by  organization administrators  instead of solely being owned by the  employee that creates them  within an organization you create  billing accounts that specify  who actually pays for google cloud  resources  you can also create folders which offer  an additional grouping mechanism for  resources  folders often represent business units  or legal entities within your company  and make it easier to organize and  monitor resource utilization  folders also act as a policy inheritance  point  access roles granted on a folder are  automatically  inherited by all projects and folders  included in that folder  now let's go a little bit deeper within  an organization  or a folder you can create projects  which form the basis for using bigquery  projects are tied to a single billing  account which means that if different  teams need to be built  individually for their bigquery usage  they would need their own  projects and additional billing accounts  would need to be created  at the organization level let's take a  look at how this resource hierarchy is  reflected in the cloud console  where you interact with and analyze your  data in bigquery  in bigquery projects are kind of like  databases in other systems  but here you can have separate projects  for storing and  analyzing your data when opening up the  console  you can see your projects on the left  hand side with the data stored  nested beneath each project name all  storage costs will be billed to the  associated projects here  even with data stored across different  projects you can analyze it together by  joining tables in a single query  at the top of the console you can see  the project where your query will  actually  run all compute costs will be billed to  this project  and its associated billing account  speaking of billing  bigquery offers two different pricing  models  projects with on-demand pricing are  charged for the number of bytes  processed by each query  but with this model there are per  project limitations and quotas  that are important to consider the other  option is flat rate pricing  where customers purchase compute  capacity to handle workloads  with this model reservations can be  assigned at the organization  folder or project levels to handle jobs  within that resource  on-demand pricing is great for getting  started with bigquery  but as your consumption grows flat rate  offers scalable compute with predictable  pricing  great so now back to the resource model  so far we've discussed resources  applicable to all google cloud  deployments  but now we'll focus on resources  specific to bigquery  within a project data sets are used to  house  organize and control access to tables  views routines and machine learning  models  they're similar to schemas in other  systems  when you create a data set you choose a  geographic location  where your data will be stored bigquery  offers a variety of regions  and multi-regions for your data  residency requirements  however you can't query across data that  lives in different locations  so make sure to plan accordingly  data sets also store metadata you can  create descriptions and labels  which can be viewed in the console or  used as tags for monitoring  you can also find details about each one  of your bigquery resources  by querying the information schema  but data sets aren't the only bigquery  resource belonging to projects  other concepts like connections and jobs  can be found here as well  we'll cover more detail on these in the  next few videos  now that you understand the top-level  containers of the bigquery resource  model  let's talk about some common ways users  structure their deployments  first off some bigquery architects  choose to create a common storage  project  that is queried in other compute  projects  in this case a centralized data platform  team can also create pipelines in this  storage project  to actually ingest data from different  sources  you can think of this storage project as  a data lake or a storage repository that  holds a bunch  of raw data then each department can  have their own project for querying the  data  saving results and creating aggregate  views  you can think of these projects as data  marts or a repository  of summarized data collected for  analysis  on a unit within the organization for  example the sales department  this structure is especially useful when  departments want to be individually  billed for their queries  or if queries need to be run in separate  projects  to avoid per-project limitations  another option is to do the reverse  where each department creates  and manages their own storage project to  house their raw data  aggregate tables or transformations of  the raw data  are then stored in a centralized data  warehouse project  where analysts can read and query the  data  this structure allows for project level  access controls against raw data  and offers a simple data access layer  for connecting business intelligence  tools to bigquery  many bigquery administrators use some  combination of these two structures  deciding on your own architecture you  should be asking questions like  how often will analysts be running  queries against these data sets  how should storage and compute be billed  to our different departments  and who should have access to what data  we hope this overview gets you well on  your way to designing your bigquery  architecture  for more guidance on getting started  with bigquery or the bigquery resource  model  check out the links below thanks for  joining us today for bigquery spotlight  watch out for the next episode and  remember stay curious  [Music]  you
 

